A1. This is hard to bear. ~ BRUNT
A6. These are litt e troubles. ~ BABES
A11. This picks up after dinner ~ WAITER
A12. What high-fliers do when they settle down. ~ ALIGHT
A14. This holds a lot of liquor—for incoming tourists. ~ BAGGAGE
A15. It pays to have these kinds of guests. ~ LODGERS
A17. A common viper. ~ ASP
A18. Humiliates. ~ HUMBLES
A20. This man's wife became a pillar in the community ~ LOT
A21. A shank. ~ SHIN
A23. These are underfoot wherever you go. ~ SOLES
A24. This has a lot of followers in Scotland Yard. ~ CLUE
A25. A native soldier of India. ~ SEPOY
A27. To make all wet. ~ RET
A28. This is full of dangerous curves. ~ SHAPE
A29. A trail early settlers traveled. ~ OREGON
A31. Extended; placed at intervals. ~ SPACED
A32. A fighting fool in Ancient Rome. ~ GLADIATOR
A33. Mohammedan chieftains. ~ SHEIKS
A36. This is always talking turkey. ~ SULTAN
A40. These make a big noise when they get the air. ~ HORNS
A41. The deadly sex personified. ~ SHE
A43. A diadem. ~ TIARA
A44. A person who does this makes a big mistake. ~ ERRS
A45. These put the wet plank in Al Smith's platform. ~ BEERS
A47. The kind of people who are born to blush unseen. ~ EBON
A48. The beginning of a new geological formation. ~ ERA
A49. A little herb of the rose family having white flowers. ~ DEWDROP
A51. Luke—in France. ~ LUC
A52. Soup. (Archaic.) ~ POTTAGE
A54. It takes a lot of this to free the slaves. ~ ALIMONY
A56. What every Irishman will be when the Ku Klux meet in Dublin. ~ RIOTER
A57. The kind of a girl Tom Thumb married. ~ NANOID
A58. College boys were all wrapped up in these last winter. ~ COATS
A59. You'll do this if you put your foot down. ~ TREAD

D1. This is one thing a Scotchman will blow. ~ BAGPIPE
D2. There was always parking space for this in grandpa's day. ~ RIG
D3. The haven for bigamists. ~ UTAH
D4. A drink made of wine. ~ NEGUS
D5. This will make you shake in your shoes. ~ TREMOR
D6. This is noted for its dancing girls. ~ BALLET
D7. A drug procured from the juice of a plant. ~ ALOES
D8. What Horatius would make at the bridge today. (Plural.) ~ BIDS
D9. When this gets broke—it runs. ~ EGG
D10. What some prohibition drinks taste like. ~ SHELLAC
D11. This makes a lot of suds. ~ WASHER
D13. This likes to make a showing. ~ TROUPE
D14. This has a deep voice. ~ BASSO
D16. This is operated by electricity in the White House. ~ STEED
D19. Stained or varnished. ~ BLEMISHED
D22. Small cups or mugs. ~ NOGGINS
D24. A famous film comedian. ~ CHARLIE
D26. These are yellow. ~ YOLKS
D28. This runs around on the roof. ~ SPOUT
D30. A well-known academy in the District of Columbia. (Init.) ~ NAS
D31. Playgrounds of city boys. (Abbr.) ~ STS
D33. A wool grower. ~ SHEEP
D34. People shudder when struck with this. ~ HORROR
D35. Eccentric. ~ ERRATIC
D37. This has a lot of scandal power. ~ TABLOID
D38. Where is the world going? ~ AROUND
D39. A feminine name. ~ NANCY
D41. These harbor a lot of criminals in Paris ~ SEWERS
D42. Roving; wandering. ~ ERRANT
D45. To cause to exist. ~ BEGET
D46. Pertaining to the sun. ~ SOLAR
D49. This is dope. ~ DATA
D50. People who do this lose weight. ~ PINE
D53. This adverb means more than enough. ~ TOO
D55. A huge extinct bird of New Zealand. ~ MOA


